and you have the wealthy living behind data communities and very separate from the way ordinary people are living and the way poor people are living. It might be psychologically easier on the middle class and the poor people not to see that. But on the other hand, it creates a greater feeling of distance between the haves and have-nots and polarization and lower levels of trust. So there's no simple answer in terms of whether we want highly visible or highly transparent inequality versus less visible and less transparent inequality because there's always a trade-off at work between what feels good versus what has negative consequences down the road. Your psychologist Keith and you know that there are several small warehouses filled with studies that show that comparing ourselves to others is often a recipe for unhappiness.

